lesson:
You've used generics since Level 1: `List<int>`, `List<string>`, `Dictionary<string, int>`. The `<T>` is a **type parameter**: the list's code was written once, and you choose what goes in it. Now you'll write your own.

**Why?** Say you want an inventory with a size limit. You could write `StringInventory`, then `ItemInventory`, then `AmmoInventory`, each a copy. Or write it once:

```
public class Inventory<T>
{
    private readonly List<T> items = new();
    public int Capacity { get; }
    public Inventory(int capacity) { Capacity = capacity; }
    public bool Add(T item)
    {
        if (items.Count >= Capacity) return false;
        items.Add(item);
        return true;
    }
}

var bag = new Inventory<string>(10);
var ammo = new Inventory<int>(3);
```

Inside the class, `T` stands for "whatever type the user picked". The compiler checks it: `bag.Add(42)` is an error, which is the whole point. Before generics people used `List<object>` and cast everything back out, and every wrong cast was a crash waiting to happen.

**Generic methods** work the same way:

```
public static List<T> Repeat<T>(T item, int times) { ... }
Util.Repeat("arrow", 3);   // T is string: the compiler works it out
```

**Constraints** say what T must be able to do. To find the largest item you must be able to compare them:

```
public static T Largest<T>(List<T> items) where T : IComparable<T>
```

Now `item.CompareTo(best)` is allowed, and `Largest(new List<int> {...})` and `Largest(new List<string> {...})` both work. Other constraints: `where T : class` (a reference type), `where T : new()` (has an empty constructor, so you can write `new T()`), `where T : Enemy` (any kind of enemy).

**The common mistake** is reaching for generics too early. Write the plain version first; make it generic when you need the same code for a second type.

In games, a classic generic is the **object pool**. Creating and destroying hundreds of bullets per second makes the game stutter, so you keep used bullets in a `Pool<Bullet>` and hand them out again. One `Pool<T>` works for bullets, sparks and enemies alike, and Unity even ships its own `ObjectPool<T>`.

example.code:
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Inventory<T>
{
    private readonly List<T> items = new();
    public int Capacity { get; }
    public Inventory(int capacity) { Capacity = capacity; }
    public int Count => items.Count;
    public bool Add(T item)
    {
        if (items.Count >= Capacity) return false;
        items.Add(item);
        return true;
    }
}

public static class Util
{
    public static T Largest<T>(List<T> items) where T : IComparable<T>
    {
        T best = items[0];
        foreach (T x in items) if (x.CompareTo(best) > 0) best = x;
        return best;
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        var bag = new Inventory<string>(2);
        Console.WriteLine($"{bag.Add("sword")} {bag.Add("shield")} {bag.Add("bow")}");
        Console.WriteLine(Util.Largest(new List<int> { 4, 19, 7 }));
        Console.WriteLine(Util.Largest(new List<string> { "maize", "beans", "sorghum" }));
    }
}

